  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1232 AM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday: \n\nHeading into Tuesday, high pressure will be moving east into the Mid \nAtlantic and New England states as a low pressure system travels \nacross southern Canada into the northern Great Lakes Region. The \nupper level trough is expected to follow the exact same track as \nthis system becomes stacked. The surface low is expected to occluded \nas it moves east before reaching the Great Lakes Region. For \nsouthern Wisconsin, the best chance for precipitation is expected to \nbe with some WAA behind the warm front and with the passage of a \ncold front. There is some uncertainty on how much if any rain/storms \nwe get with the push of WAA and any we do get will just eat away at \nany instability we would have gained in the afternoon/evening. The \nmain concern here is the lack of any low level forcing. As temps and \nmoisture increases diurnally there will be instability, but nothing \nto force convection. Which reflects why there are only a few model \nruns with any after convection so low chance but non zero. \nEspecially if one shower goes, its outflow could trigger many \nmore. Now with the passage of the cold front there is expected \nto be better upper level lit from the trough, but the timing of \nthis looks to occur during the overnight hours into Wednesday \nlate morning. This separates the best instability with the best\nlift and forcing. So while rain and storms are expected due to \nthis confidence on any strong to severe storms are low. Its a \nlow but non zero chance situation. Shear looks to be the one \ncategory that is persistent from the WAA to the passage of the \ncold front in support of stronger storms. It will just come down\nto timing and when our best forcing and instability resides. \n\nThe low pressure system and front are expected to clear southern \nWisconsin by Wednesday afternoon. High pressure is then \nexpected to return to southern Wisconsin from Manitoba on \nThursday and Friday. This area of high pressure will be exciting\nto the east Friday night as another low pressure system \napproaches from the Plains. This will bring rain chances back \nfor the weekend. An upper level trough is expected to move \nthrough Canada with a few short wave troughs moving through the \nlarger flow across the Great Lakes Region. These storms chances \nseem to largely be driven by the shortwave aloft and the LLJ. \nWhich when aligned can lead to rain and storms, but there are \nsome meso scale features that can throw that off or take a day \nof storm potential and turn it into mostly with little \nlightning. A lot of it will come down to where the best shear \nand instability reside which global models seem to be washing \nout and spreading out over a generous portion of the state. This\nis then reflected in the low chances around 10-30%. More \ndetails will shake out as we get closer to the weekend. \n\nPatterson",
